Pre-backup hooks — application consistency (#100, ADR-0009 §6)

restic-backup runs every executable *.sh in this directory, in name order, before
it takes the snapshot. A hook renders a CONSISTENT copy of one app's live state
somewhere under /data; the snapshot then picks that copy up like any other file.

Why this exists: a file-level copy of a live database is a crash-consistent copy.
For SQLite and Postgres that means "usually fine, occasionally corrupt", and the
corruption is discovered during a restore. A hook turns that into a supported
operation the database itself performs.

The contract:

  - Name it 10-<app>.sh, make it executable, keep it idempotent.
  - Write the dump under /data/<app>/ so it lands inside the backup set.
  - Exit non-zero to ABORT the whole run. That is deliberate: a snapshot taken
    without one app's consistency is exactly the backup that looks fine until it
    is needed.
  - Keep it fast. Park (#101) waits on this path.

Examples, for when the first stateful app lands (E03.5/E03.6):

  SQLite   sqlite3 /data/<app>/app.db ".backup '/data/<app>/app.db.bak'"
  Postgres docker exec <container> pg_dump -U <user> <db> > /data/<app>/dump.sql

This directory is empty today because the lab has no stateful app yet. It ships
empty on purpose rather than with speculative hooks for apps that do not exist.
